        ":question/problem": "$$\nf(t,z)=\u27e82\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z\u27e9\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$ What is the second component of $f?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1\n$$\n\nin $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\u27e8x,y\u27e9=\u27e82\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\u27e9,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(6\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)^{2}}{36} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}\u2061t+{sin}^{2}\u2061t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t,\\,z\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $6\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t.$",
